course: dictionary
lesson: 10649
title: Unique: one of a kind
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Unique means being the only one of its kind, unlike anything else. Every snowflake is unique, so no two are exactly the same. The context helps: «every zebra's pattern is unique» tells you each zebra has a pattern that no other zebra has.
explain: Unique can also mean found only in one place. Koalas are unique to Australia, so you only find them there. In «each has a throat patch as unique as a fingerprint», the words as unique as a fingerprint show that each patch is one of a kind.
explain: Unique is already special, so do not say «more unique». Informally, people also use it to mean very special or unusual, as in «a unique chance». Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
fact: Unique means being the only one of its kind.
fact: «Unique» can also mean found only in one place, as in «koalas are unique to Australia».
fact: «Fingerprints are unique», so the police can use them to identify a person.
fact: In maths, «a unique answer» means there is only one correct answer.
fact: You should not say «more unique», because unique already means one of a kind.
exercise: «Every zebra's pattern is unique.» What does unique mean here? | Hard to see | Very black and white | *Only one of its kind | Found in a zoo
exercise: «Koalas are unique to Australia.» What does unique to mean? | Loved by everyone in Australia | Bigger in Australia | *Found only in Australia | Brought there by ships
exercise: In maths, Nadia says «a unique answer» means many correct answers. What is the right meaning? | Many correct answers | A wrong answer | An answer with a big number | *Only one correct answer
test: «Each whale shark has a pattern of spots as unique as a fingerprint.» What does unique mean here? | All whale sharks look the same | Very big and heavy | Very quiet | *Each one is different from every other
test: Jayden says, «This is a truly unique chance!» What does unique mean in this informal way? | Cheap and easy | *Very special and unusual | Ordinary and boring | Lost and hard to find
exam: In PSHE lessons, what does «everyone is unique» mean? | Everyone is tired | Everyone is exactly the same | Everyone must wear the same uniform | *Each person is special and different
